      ":question/id": "b32b99fe-ff0f-5e37-86c0-43855f0e620a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 2 \\cos{(x )}=- 4 y \\sin{(x )} + 2\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 2 \\cos{(x )}&=- 4 y \\sin{(x )} + 2 \\\\ 2 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\sin{(x )}&=2 \\cos{(x )} + 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(2 \\sin{(x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{\\cos{(x )} + 1}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2 \\sin{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=\\cos{(x )} + 1.$",
