      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos{(x )}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sin{(2 x )}-\\sin{(x )}y&=0 \\\\ \\cos{(x )}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sin{(x )}y&=-\\sin{(2 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\sin{(x )}}{\\cos{(x )}})y&=-\\frac{\\sin{(2 x )}}{\\cos{(x )}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\tan{(x )})y&=-\\frac{2\\sin{(x )}\\cos{(x )}}{\\cos{(x )}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- \\tan{(x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{- 2 \\sin{(x )}}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- \\tan{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=- 2 \\sin{(x )}.$",
